{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities","id":1446544,"title":"Revenant communities","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-04-29T19:24:12-07:00","id":1446544,"title":"Revenant communities","content":"I'm curious how people describe or detail Revenant homesteads, particularly the Grimaldi and Obertus.<br /> <br /> We're told that Zantosa and Bratovitch live in family estates. Bratovitches live on around 20 with 10-20 members each. So there's around 300 Bratovitch's total. Zantosa live on &quot;at least&quot; 17 estates between 5-20 members. So maybe around 200 plus members. Large, isolated family estates easily allow these Revenants to do whatever they want outside the eyes of mortals.<br /> <br /> But the Grimaldi and Obertus are different and present some challenges for the ST.<br /> <br /> Grimaldi are supposed to live among humanity, but as individuals or presumably nuclear family units. The problem is that revenants age slowly. Not an issue for adults, but children are supposed to take 50 years to reach adulthood. It's possible for people to interact with an adult for a long time without noticing they don't age normally. You can probably spend one to decades in the same place before moving on. But if children don't age, it'll be noticed quickly. That does not present a problem for Zantosa and Bratovitch who live isolated from humans, and can do various things to limit their interaction with them. Much harder for the Grimaldi. How do you handle this in the setting as the ST?<br /> <br /> Obertus have another issue. They do seem to live in isolation from humanity, but instead of living on family estates they gather in villages and even govern themselves &quot;normally&quot; like having municipal elections and such. The issue here isn't hiding among humanity. They probably keep outsiders out of their communities and create any false documents as needed like birth and death certificates. The issue is given the small number of Revenants, the Obertus likely number in the same range as Bratvotiches and Zantosa. Let's be generous and give them 500. How many villages can they possibly have? We're told they can be found in New England, the Midwest, and Central America. Does that mean all Obertus live in a total of three villages - one in each of those regions - of around 150-170 members each? It's possible to have villages this size, but they are very rare. There wouldn't be many places of business or public buildings. You could still theoretically elect a village president or mayor, and even a council, but they wouldn't have a whole lot to do. We can make it work, but the greater issue is should there really only be three villages total to account for the entirety of this revenant family - let's call them Dunwich, Gatlin, and Mayincateca for convenience. I imagine in addition to being remote, they are also isolated by geography in someway to prevent outsiders from stumbling upon them by accident and making the region around them unattractive for development. As home to around one-third of the whole revenant family (as opposed to just a clan) you can't just pick up and flee. You have to make yourself a hard target. Is that how you see it in your chronicles?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T20:26:43-07:00","id":1446551,"title":"","content":"I kind of always saw the Obertus (and really all the families) being a little more scattered, living in creepy rundown and mostly forgotten estates similar to the Addams Family, some of Shirley Jackson's work (Haunting of Hill House and We Have Always Lived in the Castle), and Gormenghast from Mervin Peake's Titus Groan. And then those who do live in villages or small towns may have some sleeping Tzimisce elder the site is centered around, and those places end up being like Lovecraft's Innsmouth or Dunwich, Stephen King's Derry, and even Summerisle from The Wicker Man. Maybe also Hogsmeade from Harry Potter if you changed it from fantasy to creepy folk horror. There's probably some still centered around these totally forgotten Greek or Balkan monasteries or small villages (which may or may not draw some inspiration from Clive Barker's &quot;In the Hills, The Cities&quot;).<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T20:55:56-07:00","id":1446552,"title":"","content":"The Addams family is one example that comes to mind regarding how Revenants live, obviously rich with a fancy house, yet its old and dilapidated. The family gets together for the big reunion and ancestral songs and dancing. Another good example good be the villains in season 1 of True Detective. Also, there are some real families that have been discovered living in complete isolation for generation, engaging in incest over those generations. Very strange, but true.<br /> <br /> As for Bratovitches, I see them living like Hillbillies. Deliverance maybe. I\u2019m from Appalachia and sometimes I\u2019ll drive through a community and think, hey this is a Bratovitch community.<br /> <br /> When it comes to Revenant children, I have always ignored the thing about taking decades for them to mature. I never liked that idea with D&amp;D elves, who could still be toddlers at an age where humans are full grown, and I don\u2019t like it with Revenants. Aging and growing are two different processes. Slower aging doesn\u2019t mean slower growth.<br /> <br /> Anyway, even if you go with slow growth, all Revenant children are going to either be homeschooled, taught by tutors, or educated in some kind of special place designed to accompany them. In any case, the kids will be removed from mainstream society until adulthood, even if they will be expected to go out some day and enter the mortal world.<br /> <br /> For the Grimaldi, I see their lifestyles and dwellings as being the closest to normal human rich people. They are probably going to want mansions with some degree of isolation, as many rich humans do, but likely also maintain homes in the city for when they must do business there. <br /> <br /> I could see those Obertus villages as being these spooky sorts of places where things seem off. Like Wicket Man as NOoC says. Obertus, even preferring isolation, will likely want to have some interaction with human population centers because that is often where knowledge is concentrated and they are a scholarly family.<br /> <br /> I see the Zantosa as being the most like the Addams family. Rich but debased, removed from mainstream society yet still tenuously connected through various means. Really perverted too, which is played for laughed until the Addams family, but if it was played straight it would be pretty disturbing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T21:48:47-07:00","id":1446555,"title":"","content":"I tend to think of Agatha Christie meets the House of Usher. Revenants live in places full of horrible secrets like incest, murder, adultery, secrets, rape, horrific abuse, lies, and more with money never an issue. Every house is full of secret passageways and you'll find the basement probably leads to an underground church where torch-lit ceremonies are held to venerate Caine (or Cthulhu or whatever).<br /> <br /> Maybe also some Innsmouth too.<br /> <br /> &quot;The family had the Grimaldi look.&quot;<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T22:03:51-07:00","id":1446558,"title":"","content":"The Bratovitch are pretty much the family from the original Texas Chainsaw Massacre, complete with the Ed Gein inspired decor. Also the whole Sawney Bean legend. So for them I've always imagined run down rural farm houses in the middle of nowhere, forgotten mining ghost towns, swamp shacks, and old smuggler dens. And more modernly, places like Baltimore's &quot;Billytown&quot; neighborhood, with underground dog fighting rings.<br /> <br /> I've always saw The Godfather as a good inspiration for the Grimaldi.<br /> <br /> And another village possibility: Children of the Corn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 04-30-2021, 01:12 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-29T23:46:57-07:00","id":1446566,"title":"","content":"You could have Revenants age more like Dhampirs, in that the slowed ageing only kicks in at puberty. It makes things a little easier.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T00:04:39-07:00","id":1446567,"title":"","content":"I don't necessarily mind the slowed aging of the children? Why? Because I think of it as a justification for the evil children cliche. Little girls who want to torture and murder you or Children of the Corn style priests.<br /> <br /> It's just they're 60 so no problem shooting them.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T01:19:46-07:00","id":1446571,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446567#post1446567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don't necessarily mind the slowed aging of the children? Why? Because I think of it as a justification for the evil children cliche. Little girls who want to torture and murder you or Children of the Corn style priests.<br /> <br /> It's just they're 60 so no problem shooting them.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I quite like it too. And the demonic pregnancies that last years.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T02:37:05-07:00","id":1446574,"title":"","content":"I don\u2019t have anything new to contribute but I love this thread. Thanks. (Except that my cousin goes to college near Baltimore. I\u2019ll have to ask her if she\u2019s ever heard of Billytown.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T03:59:53-07:00","id":1446581,"title":"","content":"Is it stated somewhere that revenants age slower from birth? Cause I could have sworn I read a reference last year to this only starting in early adulthood. Having no success finding a direct statement to that effect in Ghouls &amp; Revenants though. What I did find however is a Bratovitch sample character being described as having grown to 6 feet by age 10. He's mentioned on page 117.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2198-malkavian87","name":"Malkavian87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2198_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T09:07:14-07:00","id":1446612,"title":"","content":"The revenant\u2019s extended childhood is another of those things that puts a lie to the \u201cdhampirs and revenants are basically the same thing, just culturally different\u201d statements.<br /> <br /> Having your physiological and cognitive development in sync until you reach young adulthood is a big deal for normal development. A Dhampir who looks twenty is probably in their mid-thirties at most and could still present themselves as their chronological age without difficulty (good genes are a thing) and would only really start getting implausible when they reached some point in their 40s.<br /> <br /> By contrast, because congnitive development is more a matter of experience forming synaptic pathways rather than physiological changes to the brain over time, it means that a revenant\u2019s physical development will always lag significantly behind their cognition. Basically, a five year old revenant will have known HOW to speak and move their limbs in a coordinated enough fashion to walk for years before their bodies are physically developed enough to allow them to actually do so. The revenant who looks seven years old could have the academic side of being a brain surgeon completed even as their body is still working out the fine motor skills needed to actually perform the acts. The 40 year old revenant looks 8-10 years old and is still a couple decades away from hitting puberty.<br /> <br /> Basically, just on biology alone revenants are going to be messed up in the head. Throw in spending all of that time they\u2019re developing surrounded by a culture based on servitude to blood drinking immortals and, frankly, there are probably Umbrood whose mental processes are more comprehensible to mortal humans (or to normal fledgling/neonate vampires for that matter).<br /> <br /> Now, one thing that could be a game-changer of sorts for younger revenants is the explosion of social media/online identities where you never need show your face in the past couple decades. A revenant who looks 5, but is cognitively in their early 20\u2019s can hang with online friends in an MMO or post funny memes to their Twitter friends just like any other 20-something can (voice chat would need some thought, though just being able to discuss topics at a level of complexity expected of an adult, a mediocre mic and a little modulation will remove just about any suspicions).<br /> <br /> In other words, it\u2019s possible that the era of socially maladjusted revenants might be waning as the early adopters of internet forums, MMO\u2019s and chat rooms are probably in their 40\u2019s by now and will finally be physically mature enough to pass as a (young) adult in another decade or two.<br /> <br /> This would particularly be true if the main physiological element of brain development (neural plasticity) was similarly extended. The short version of which is that plasticity refers to the ability of the brain to adapt to changes. Children\u2019s brains are far more plastic than adults and reduced plasticity is one of the reasons people get more set in their ways and are less able to adapt to changes in technology or cultural trends.<br /> <br /> But if Revenants maintain brain plasticity in tune with their physiological age, they\u2019d still be able to keep up with evolving trends and technology until well past the chronological century mark. A 60-75 year old revenant would be able to keep up with all the changes just like a human 15 year old can... and will probably still be able to adapt even as the teenage mortal friends they made at 70 are yelling about youngster needing to get off their lawns.<br /> <br /> A Revenant as a social chameleon who broke free of their family at, say, 75 (looking 15-ish) is now 120 (looks mid-twenties) and has been hanging out in human subcultures since the mid-70s (i.e. back when getting a job right out of high school was normal and 15 could pass as a baby-faced 18-20) is absolutely a viable concept if you run with the brain plasticity angle.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T12:01:43-07:00","id":1446641,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446566#post1446566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could have Revenants age more like Dhampirs, in that the slowed ageing only kicks in at puberty. It makes things a little easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm thinking this may be a better option. It at least allows the Grimaldi live amongst humans. I don't think there's issues with prolonged childhood with the other more isolationist breeds, but even here over the course of thousands of years interacting with humanity, it would probably cause some problems.<br /> <br /> For STs who want the creepy child with experience and wisdom far beyond her appearance, that can still be done with a Flaw. And given the strange genetics of Revenants, that may even be a common one.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malkavian87</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446581#post1446581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it stated somewhere that revenants age slower from birth?</div> </div> </div> </div>Ghouls: Fatal Addiction mentions that. I haven't found references to that in earlier books, so perhaps that book introduced the concept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T13:34:06-07:00","id":1446652,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446551#post1446551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I kind of always saw the Obertus (and really all the families) being a little more scattered, living in creepy rundown and mostly forgotten estates...</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that's a fine option as long as you don't mind going against canon. I change lots of things from canon so I don't feel bound to it.<br /> <br /> But if one does go with canon, it seems to mean that there are probably only three Obertus communities in the entire world, maybe one or two more if you lower the village population even more.<br /> <br /> Strangely enough, if that is true we have the name of the Obertus settlement in New England. The NPC description of Ginevra Salamanca in Ghouls: Fatal Addiction says she is actually Ginevra Obertus from the Obertus community of Haverhill, MA. So we have our &quot;Dunwich&quot; in the game. I wonder if there have been any other name drops of Obertus communities that would prove problematic?<br /> <br /> In actuality, I don't think this was intended. I am pretty sure this is just another example of someone being inspired by an idea without thinking through the details into practicalities. The limited number of Revenants implies there just can't be more than a few Obertus towns.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T15:41:12-07:00","id":1446672,"title":"","content":"How should the Revenant families best be used? There seems to me to be a difference between how Revenants are usually depicted in games versus how they were originally described.<br /> <br /> In the original ST's Guide to the Sabbat we were told that while the Bratovitch, Zantosa, and Grimaldi knew of each other, that the Obertus were almost totally unknown. Later sourcebooks ignored this and portrayed things as if they all knew of each other. It's purely a matter of taste, but I like the idea that the Obertus were practically unknown.<br /> <br /> While the Revenant families once provided the Tzimisce and Sabbat with valuable retainers, spies, and recruits in previous times, by now the Revenant families don't serve much real purpose to the Sabbat and are not destroyed simply because the Tzimisce consider them part of their culture and are loathe to see any more harm done to them. Since 4 other Revenant families loyal to the Sabbat have already been destroyed, we should keep in mind how much they are loathed. In actual game play, the impression I get is that the Revenants are a much more utilized resource for the Sabbat, likely because STs just want to do something with the concept. Much like how the Kiasyd, explicitly not of the Sabbat, somehow became members of the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> But I do think the Revenant families provide some some utility to the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> I think the Bratovitch serve two purposes. First, their estates make good spots for nomad packs in North America to visit as way stations while they travel. They are a known safe space in a hostile wilderness of Lupines and Camarilla Archons/surveillance (I imagine there are several Archon packs similar to the Wolfpack of Chicago By Night that patrol various areas of North America). Bratovitch husbandry also provides lots of animal blood and perhaps human depending on how recently some poor travelers came across one of their estates.<br /> <br /> Despite their reputation for being Lupine hunters, I think it should be obvious that most Bratovitch would be slaughtered going against a Lupine pack, and even fighting a lone Lupine would be risky and hazardous. And given their low numbers, they can't risk a destructive fight that might bring one or more packs that could wipe out an entire estate. The loss of such members would be catastrophic. But I don't think that means they don't serve some anti-Lupine role. First, Bratovitch likely have their estates in areas NOT known to have Lupines. So their presence indicates the area is safe. Second, there is likely enough hand me down lore that is still useful for them to identify evidence of nearby Lupine activity. They can recognize spor, have unique dog breeds that recognize scent, and they probably have lots of silver weapons on hand.<br /> <br /> If they actually kill any Lupine near their estates, it'd be the equivalent of lone Ronin and Lost Cubs. Maybe a travelling Silent Strider exploring an area after a long while. In so far that they might attack an actual small sept of Lupines that represent a small number of Garou, I imagine it happens like this. First, the various Bratovitch estates do a lot of scouting. They explore the wilderness a lot and travel far away from their homes in order to know what areas are really dangerous or relatively safe. Maybe they might need to relocate some day. Or maybe it's just good information to have to share with Sabbat some time. Second, at some point they actually find a small sept of Lupines. And when that happens, the various Bratovitch clans plan an attack. They salvage abandoned automobiles from the junkyard, put false license plates on them, and coordinate a very large group to this small and isolated group of Lupines. They do as much necessary to stack the deck in their favor, and to hide anything on them that might be used to locate where they came from so if things go bad, the Lupines can't track them back home. Such attacks probably don't happen very often. Maybe not even once a generation, maybe only once or twice a century. And the Lupines killed aren't many - maybe 3-6 against 20-50 Bratovitches plus ghouled dog packs that attack them. But because they do succeed, they brag it up and they have that reputation.<br /> <br /> The Zantosa are supposed to be almost entirely useless. Part of that was the metaplot reason that some of them were guardians of the Tzimisce Antedeluvian and thus actual traitors to the Sabbat. But even those loyal don't provide much. Having estates, they too can provide spots in the wilderness for nomad packs and others to camp out temporarily. However, I think they also provide another benefit that fits their debauched reputations. They probably act as procurers to the Sabbat for normal humans used in ritae like the Blood Feast when the big Sabbat gatherings occur. Their degenerate ways using drugs and sex likely enable them to find and groom lots of willing victims who don't learn what will really happen to them until too late.<br /> <br /> The Obertus likely do research for only a small handful of elder Tzimisce who need obscure lore researched. That might include some kolduns. And the Obertus might be unacknowledged cocreators of a lot of Tzimisce experiments like the Blood Brothers. They might make a somewhat effective, though minor replacement for the loss of House Goratrix in terms of Tzimisce thaumaturgical research. Why shouldn't Obertus not be able to use Blood Sorcery if they have vitae of their own? And possible that some of their members might be Infernalists - not outright on the Path of Evil Revelations, but in the sense of knowing some Dark Thaumaturgy and trying to outwit the devil. Outside of this, they are likely left alone and work on their own goals.<br /> <br /> Grimaldi of course are charged with providing for the Masquerade needs of the Sabbat and providing the archbishops they are blood bonded to with cash. This is actually an interesting statement, as wouldn't the vaulderie destroy such bonds? Even if the Grimaldi don't know or practice that rite themselves (though since they have their own vitae, it's possible they could do so), the vaulderie is very open knowledge in the Sabbat. Needless to say, relying on the blood bond in a sect based on destroying such things appears to be an error. I imagine somewhere in the Grimaldi larger family, someone knows how to perform the vaulderie and passes that knowledge on. Maybe long thought dead and unknown to the Sabbat. They're just waiting for the perfect moment. I think it was part of the Revised metaplot that the Grimaldi were to defect to the Ventrue. Since the Grimaldi are so closely tied to the Sabbat bishops, I imagine they are only present in cities where the Sabbat is in power. They could also be disguised as personal ghouls to Sabbat infiltrators who can pass messages along until they reach the Sabbat bishops. Needless to say, all these bishops should be Tzimisce since they'd be loathed by other Sabbat. So this gives the Tzimisce a lot of indirect influence that balances out what the Lasombra may have.<br /> <br /> None of this is new - all pulled from existing material. But hopefully explored in more detail.<br /> <br /> However, I think overall the Revenant families shouldn't be very prominent even in a Sabbat setting. They are more of a background element unless the ST has a particular plot element that utilizes them heavily. But I think they can be used to explain or justify certain strange things in the setting that might even show up in other games.<br /> <br /> I think their main issue from an ST perspective, is that their relatively small numbers mean if a plot goes sideways, a significant portion of them could get wiped out and altering the game world setting to a large degree. That's not necessarily a bad thing, but many STs want to preserve these elements for further use, not have a chronicle detailing why 1/3 of the Obertus suddenly died and having the repercussions of which hijack the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T15:52:39-07:00","id":1446675,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446652#post1446652\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that's a fine option as long as you don't mind going against canon. I change lots of things from canon so I don't feel bound to it.<br /> <br /> But if one does go with canon, it seems to mean that there are probably only three Obertus communities in the entire world, maybe one or two more if you lower the village population even more.<br /> <br /> Strangely enough, if that is true we have the name of the Obertus settlement in New England. The NPC description of Ginevra Salamanca in Ghouls: Fatal Addiction says she is actually Ginevra Obertus from the Obertus community of Haverhill, MA. So we have our &quot;Dunwich&quot; in the game. I wonder if there have been any other name drops of Obertus communities that would prove problematic?<br /> <br /> In actuality, I don't think this was intended. I am pretty sure this is just another example of someone being inspired by an idea without thinking through the details into practicalities. The limited number of Revenants implies there just can't be more than a few Obertus towns.</div> </div> </div> </div>I expect that it suits the family to work together on their research. If nothing else, they can share the same libraries. Plus it saves them the hassle of courtship.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3523-spencer-from-the-hills","name":"Spencer from The Hills","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"546"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T18:19:05-07:00","id":1446709,"title":"","content":"I definitely think libraries are one of the few public buildings in town for an Obertus community. Probably no popular fiction or YA novels, but lots of scientific reference books, unexpurgated classics (often in the original Greek or Latin), and the entire non-abridged set of The Golden Bough and similar mythological, religious, or occult works.<br /> <br /> Though all the good stuff is still in private hands.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T19:47:45-07:00","id":1446720,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Penelope</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446574#post1446574\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I don\u2019t have anything new to contribute but I love this thread. Thanks. (Except that my cousin goes to college near Baltimore. I\u2019ll have to ask her if she\u2019s ever heard of Billytown.)</div> </div> </div> </div>When the war industry ramped up at the start of WW2, a lot of poor people moved from the Appalachian mountains to Baltimore for work, and the part of town they settled in started being called Hillybilly Town, later shortened to Billytown. Like most ethno-cultural neighborhoods, the behavior of the residents varies a lot, but it has had a reputation for things like people raising goats and chickens in their front yards, cock fighting and dog fighting, meth labs, opioid addiction, and the like. Sort of like the tv show Justified transplanted to the city where The Wire and Homicide: Life on the Street took place. <br /> <br /> In the case of the Bratovitch, I like to think there was probably a small family of them who moved to the city in the 40s, who've been this violent little clan of sociopaths involved in all the worst aspects of the neighborhood's culture, probably complete with a bunch of bodies buried under the crawlspace.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-04-30T23:03:51-07:00","id":1446747,"title":"","content":"I've yet to use revenant families in my games in any substantive capacity. But I like the idea of them, and they're there if I want to do anything with them.<br /> <br /> I don't think the Grimaldi slowed aging is a particularly big handicap. If their Tzimisce masters really want to, they can just fleshcraft a revenant to make their biological age match their actual age. Vicissitude isn't in-clan for the family, but there are probably a few who've learned it anyway. Their shtick is supposed to be upholding the Masquerade and it's too useful a Discipline for <i>someone</i> not to pick up.<br /> <br /> Beyond that, the family probably has their walled estates in posh neighborhoods where discretion is just another commodity their wealth can purchase. Children are home-schooled and sent to elite colleges once they look old enough to pass as members of the student body. Some may not bother, but they're supposed to be Masquerade experts and many probably maintain mortal identities. They pick up credentials, go to work at family-connected institutions, and stick around until their slowed aging becomes too obvious to conceal via makeup. Once that happens, or even if they get help from Vicissitude but enough years go by, they probably retire that identity, forge a new one, and pop up in another city with a Grimaldi presence as &quot;Cousin X from out of town.&quot; Having the support infrastructure of an extended family is a big asset there.<br /> <br /> In my setting, the Grimaldi family replace some of the real-life <a href=\"https://www.forbes.com/sites/kerryadolan/2021/04/06/mexicos-richest-billionaires-2021/?sh=335163c15265\" target=\"_blank\">wealthiest people</a> in Mexico. Telecom feels like something the Technocracy would sink its claws into, but mining or retail are good sources for the Grimaldi wealth in the country. Given how old and widespread they are, they probably get their wealth from a lot of sources, like, uh, also being the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monarchy_of_Monaco\" target=\"_blank\">royal family of Monaco.</a> If I ever used them in a game, I'd replace Albert II with a fictional analogue to avoid 5e's mistake.<br /> <br /> <i>A World of Darkness 2e</i> also did something with the Grimaldis in Monaco. I don't remember if they presented the family's rule over the country being a secret or not. If it is, then &quot;Grimaldi&quot; probably is not the name that most vampires know them by. Then again, maybe not. The Sabbat is supposed to be an incredibly insular and secretive organization, and most Camarilla vampires probably can't name any of the revenant families. Much like the Obertus being a secret, &quot;the Sabbat is extremely secretive&quot; was an idea that setting books presented as fact but never really followed up on. I can't think of any canon instances where Camarilla vampires were especially ignorant of the Sabbat's history or culture at an inconvenient time. (This was supposed to be what made infiltrating the sect so hard, along with, obviously, the vaulderie.)<br /> <br /> I don't think the Sabbat would be inclined to include mere revenants in vaulderies, much less share the knowledge of how to perform independent vaulderies. The vaulderie is the heart of Sabbat culture and I don't think packs would see revenants as worthy to participate in that, even if they're a step up from human ghouls. I also don't like the idea of revenants/ghouls being able to &quot;contribute&quot; their blood towards vaulderies. I have no idea whether that's possible or not under V20's rules, but from a story perspective it doesn't make any sense; they already can't use their blood to bond other people. If a revenant participated in a vaulderie, it'd break their old blood bonds (and establish new vinculums), but that'd be it. They'd drain from the communal chalice without giving anything back.<br /> <br /> I like the Zantosas being procurers of vessels. Many Sabbat packs actively enjoy night-to-night hunting, but archbishops and other Sabbat luminaries probably turn to the Zantosas for help when they need lots of vessels for a blood feast.<br /> <br /> I don't like the Bratovitch reputation as werewolf hunters being essentially a load of hot air. From a story perspective/game mechanics aside, I like the idea that almost any individual revenant should lose against a Garou, but a well-coordinated team of revenants armed with silver should be a serious threat to lone werewolves. You (<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar3046_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"3046\" data-vbnamecard=\"3046\">Black Fox</a>) are probably right that most of their reputation comes from taking down lone Garou or rookie packs rather than seasoned ones, though.<br /> <br /> One thing that I don't remember lore answering anywhere is where, exactly, Obertus revenants come from. They obviously owe their origins to the Dracon's descendants in Constantinople, many of whom had ties to the city's monastic community. So when and why did those vampires start breeding revenants? Why aren't they simply Tzimisce descendants of the Dracon?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T07:32:30-07:00","id":1446793,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446720#post1446720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> When the war industry ramped up at the start of WW2, a lot of poor people moved from the Appalachian mountains to Baltimore for work, and the part of town they settled in started being called Hillybilly Town, later shortened to Billytown. Like most ethno-cultural neighborhoods, the behavior of the residents varies a lot, but it has had a reputation for things like people raising goats and chickens in their front yards, cock fighting and dog fighting, meth labs, opioid addiction, and the like. Sort of like the tv show Justified transplanted to the city where The Wire and Homicide: Life on the Street took place. <br /> <br /> In the case of the Bratovitch, I like to think there was probably a small family of them who moved to the city in the 40s, who've been this violent little clan of sociopaths involved in all the worst aspects of the neighborhood's culture, probably complete with a bunch of bodies buried under the crawlspace.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wow! I love it. Thanks.<br /> <br /> I love revenants in general. The Bratovitch, Grimaldi, Obertus and Zantosa families are all great portrayals of different aspects of the revenant experience. I could even see like an all-revenant coterie with characters from different families working together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"","name":"Guest","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/default/default_avatar_large.png","title":"","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T09:11:26-07:00","id":1446800,"title":"","content":"I've sometimes wondered what happens when members of two revenant families breed. Its got to happen now and then. They do have a lot in common and generally seem to have a pretty broad appreciation for sex.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446672#post1446672\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Grimaldi of course are charged with providing for the Masquerade needs of the Sabbat and providing the archbishops they are blood bonded to with cash. This is actually an interesting statement, as wouldn't the vaulderie destroy such bonds? Even if the Grimaldi don't know or practice that rite themselves (though since they have their own vitae, it's possible they could do so), the vaulderie is very open knowledge in the Sabbat. Needless to say, relying on the blood bond in a sect based on destroying such things appears to be an error. I imagine somewhere in the Grimaldi larger family, someone knows how to perform the vaulderie and passes that knowledge on. Maybe long thought dead and unknown to the Sabbat. They're just waiting for the perfect moment. I think it was part of the Revised metaplot that the Grimaldi were to defect to the Ventrue.</div> </div> </div> </div>I figure the Grimaldi have to end up working with the Kings and Queens of Shadow a lot, and that they'll both defect at the same time, taking the lions share of the Sabbats wealth and resources with them.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2471-trollroot","name":"Trollroot","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"311"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T10:24:27-07:00","id":1446808,"title":"","content":"I've been eager to use Revenants for a family of high class canibals in a hunter game for a while now. Alas, I don't have a hunter game running. <br /> <br /> RE slow growth; It makes sense. If you embrace a kid, they stay a kid. Vitae is something that suspends your body in it's state at the time of consumption, not something that specifically targets aging. (it's more complex than that, but anyhow) <br /> <br /> True horror is subjecting a woman to more than half a decade of breastfeeding the same infant, and then trying for another child. I'm sure the revenants would be behind the formula industry. ( which would explain some of their ethical lapses) <br /> <br /> <br /> All the kids are homeschooled. which means the crazy is passed down and amplified through the generations. It might be humourous to set up an all-revenant private school; I imagine that'd make gladiators out of kids. A juvie for rich people.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T18:31:50-07:00","id":1446881,"title":"","content":"When Revenants were introduced we were told that original 8 families were loyal to the Sabbat, but the Sabbat eventually destroyed four of them before the Tzimisce put the survivors under their own protection. But I don't think we've ever been told who those other 4 families were or the circumstances surrounding their destruction.<br /> <br /> Eventually we got a lot more Revenant families named during the Tzimisce clanbooks and Dark Ages line, but so far as I know, none of them ever had a destiny of entering the Sabbat and being destroyed by them. They were either destroyed while never entering the Sabbat, created afterwards, or joined some other faction.<br /> <br /> Did I just miss that somewhere, or has the game never defined them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T20:05:03-07:00","id":1446893,"title":"","content":"I think the ones in Transylvania by Night were supposed to be them. It is probably one of those instances where as Developers changed and parts of the history were fleshed out (especially in the historical lines), certain things get changed, clarified, retconned, etc. This is fairly common in the rpg industry, especially with games/settings that get more than a two dozen books over a ten year period.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T20:31:31-07:00","id":1446898,"title":"","content":"The Vlaszy, Basarabs, and Danislav were all detailed in Transylvania and wiped out around the Anarch Revolt. I don't remember which family was the fourth one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T21:26:12-07:00","id":1446907,"title":"","content":"I want to say it was the one that defected to the Tremere, but I don't have my books handy to look.<br /> <br /> The Basarabs were actually a real world political lineage in Wallachia (their main rivals being the Danesti). Vlad II and Vlad III (the Impaler) are from the house, and they held the throne until the 1600s.<br /> This is similar to the Grimaldi also being a real world noble house, originating in Genoa, Italy and ruling of Monaco since around the 14th century or so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-01T21:43:17-07:00","id":1446910,"title":"","content":"The Krevcheski rebelled, went into hiding, and rebranded themselves as the Ducheski family in service to the Tremere. Perhaps they were intended to be the fourth &quot;destroyed&quot; family?<br /> <br /> I think the Oprichniki have only ever served Old Clan Tzimisce from Eastern Europe since their founding, they never would have been affiliated with the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> I agree with False Epiphany that people are probably only read into the specifics of the Vaulderie when they become True Sabbat, and the ceremony shouldn't work for non-vampires even if they learned its secrets somehow.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-02T01:06:14-07:00","id":1446926,"title":"","content":"Obviously if those Revenant families were destroyed during the Anarch Revolt, they could not have joined the Sabbat to be destroyed later on. The Krechevski/Duchevski who allied with the Tremere obviously also did not join the Sabbat (and were already mentioned to have joined the Tremere in the original Revenant write up). These were all families who were not among the eight who did join the Sabbat. So it sounds like those four were never detailed. If the writers intended the Basarab, Khavi, Vlazy, Danislav etc. to have been those Revenant families they seemingly forgot they were supposed to have joined the Sabbat first.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, the original description of the Revenants made it clear that besides the eight who joined the Sabbat, there were lots of other families that did not survive the Anarch Revolt, became independent, or became affiliated with one of the other clans. So I don't believe the mention of the Basarab, Vlazy, etc. being destroyed in the Anarch Revolt was a screw up by people. There's been far more than eight families that have been defined. It seems the writers just forgot to ever mention the additional four who joined the Sabbat and were later wiped out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 11-05-2021, 11:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-02T06:30:14-07:00","id":1446964,"title":"","content":"I think the writers may have meant for those families to be the destroyed ones, but just screwed up and forgot they were supposed to have joined the Sabbat before being wiped out by the Sabbat.<br /> <br /> The Basarabs potentially work as a family destroyed by the Sabbat after joining the Sabbat, since as <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar12890_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12890\" data-vbnamecard=\"12890\">No One of Consequence</a> notes, they continued to rule Wallachia into the 1600s. Vlad Tepes was supposed to have joined the Sabbat at some point after his Embrace but probably before his appearance in Transylvania Chronicles III during the 1700s, where he doesn't seem to be a member of the sect, and is explicitly mentioned as having later defected to the Camarilla before going independent. One could conjecture the Sabbat was either ignorant of or willing to overlook his diablerie of Tabak, but that after he turned traitor, they killed his mortal family in retaliation (potentially with help from non-Sabbat Tzimisce elders who didn't want the Basarabs to produce another Dracula either).<br /> <br /> Looking it up, the Danislavs were apparently destroyed before the Anarch Revolt too, after they turned to worship of Grandfather Thunder and overthrew their Tzimisce master, incurring the wrath of the rest of the clan.<br /> <br /> Canon is rarely consistent, anyway. One could have there only be four big name destroyed families, or invent three more to have been destroyed by the Sabbat alongside the Basarabs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":3110,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","user":"False Epiphany","date":". 05-02-2021, 09:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-02T12:19:55-07:00","id":1447016,"title":"","content":"The new revenant families mentioned in Libellus Sanguinus 1 were:<ul><li>Vlazy - remained loyal to the elders and destroyed during the Anarch Revolt.</li> <li>Khavi - wiped out by the Teutonic Knights possibly before the Anarch Revolt or during its early stages. Loyal to their methusaleh until they were destroyed.</li> </ul>It is also mentioned that besides these &quot;major strains&quot; (which includes the 4 known Sabbat families and the Krechevski/Duchevski) there are minor families like the Rustovitch and Ruthvenski. I imagine this was mentioned in order to give STs and PCs options to include additional revenant families in their backstories and chronicles without violating canon.<br /> <br /> The new revenant families mentioned in Transylvania By Night. Since they were not mentioned in Libellus Sanguinus 1, we could say they are further examples of minor families. Though it is said these are &quot;other families of note&quot; which was likely intended to be &quot;oh, these are major strains as well, just ignore that bit in LS1 that there were only 7 major strains, we'll introduce more whenever we want.&quot;<ul><li>Basarab - none survive by the 16th century. The 16th century begins at 1500 (or 1501 for you pendants out there). The Convention of Thorns happens in 1493. The Sabbat as an organization did not begin to form until afterwards, and wasn't anything we'd consider to be recognizable until Vestgeir saved the sect. Dracula was embraced in 1495 (the historical Dracula was long dead by then). So the Basarab were quickly wiped out before there could be a real Sabbat.</li> <li>Danislav - betrayed the Tzimisce using powers derived from their kinfolk status to the Shadow Lords, and subsequently destroyed by the Tzimisce. Since we're told the rebellion happened in the mid-14th century, and several Tzimisce united in 1399 to destroy them, they were likely destroyed very early during the Anarch Revolt.</li> </ul>So we have the names of at least 6 other Revenant families and know there are other unnamed minor ones. In other sourcebooks, we'll be given even more Tzimisce Revenant families.<br /> <br /> I think it's a stretch of the imagination to say these four were meant to be the families that entered the Sabbat and were destroyed by them. They just fit the original idea that the Tzimisce Revenants had lots of various fates after the Anarch Revolt. Some were loyal to the elders and destroyed; some became independent or found new masters; and eight entered the Sabbat. At best only the Basarab (out of six known new families) might count as a revenant family that supported the Anarchs and thus be considered to have joined the Sabbat though this is not technically correct. However, this is just supposition as we're not told (as far as I know) that the Basarab sided with the Anarchs. It also doesn't fit the idea that other non-Tzimisce Sabbat wiped out the revenant families prompting the Tzimisce to save the others, as it were the Tzimisce themselves who destroyed the Basarab (and even Danislav).<br /> <br /> So none of the four named families fit into what we know about the unnamed Sabbat families that were destroyed.<br /> <br /> I think it's much more likely the writers either forgot entirely there were supposed to be four more Sabbat revenant families, or purposely left them out intending to develop them later or to remain a mystery.<br /> <br /> Everyone's tastes are different though.<br /> <br /> I'll likely need to create my own four Sabbat revenant families. I usually run historical chronicles (even if that means I begin my games in the 1920s, 50s, 70s, etc. before they reach &quot;today&quot;) so it's entirely possible I might have a need for one of these destroyed families. But it's not a high priority.<br /> <br /> However, the names dropped for the minor families of Rustovitch and Ruthvenski are likely important. We know the Tzimisce Vladimir Rustovitch becomes a Sabbat (although he is confusingly called a Bratovitch Revenant). We also know there was a line of powerful Tzimisce vampires which included Ruthven in their name, including the Anarch Lambech Ruthven. It's possible they followed these vampires into the Sabbat. Otherwise it's a curiosity they share the name of prominent Sabbat vampires. While their members might have married into other families at points, it's entirely possible that the &quot;main line&quot; of these families were the ones destroyed by other Sabbat. So I might just need to create the names of another two.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 11-05-2021, 11:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T08:41:40-07:00","id":1447204,"title":"","content":"Hmm.<br /> <br /> Bratovitch: Animalism, Potence, Vicissitude = savage rural animal trainers (abusers)<br /> <br /> Obertus: Auspex, Obfuscate, Vicissitude = creepy reclusive arcane scholars<br /> <br /> Zanatosa: Auspex, Presence, Vicissitude = sleazy wealthy hedonistic lechers<br /> <br /> Grimaldi: Celerity, Dominate, Fortitude = having little to do with the Tzimisce<br /> <br /> (OK, that's not entirely fair. But I've honestly always felt like this family was created - by the writer and developer out of game, not by vampires in game - just to fill the gap left by the Sabbat PG not giving them background dots and making Sabbat ghouls rarer than hen's teeth. The fact that they have no Disciplines in common with the Tzimisce, but two in common with the Ventrue of all clans, only adds to this. In hindsight, I kind of wish they had Auspex, Dominate, and Fortitude, which would at least tie them to the Old Clan Tzimisce a little more. I also wish they had a different name. But that's just me.)<br /> <br /> Grimaldi = wealthy elites eternally trapped in The Family Business<br /> <br /> Duscheski: Auspex, Dominate, Thaumaturgy = Tremere<br /> <br /> (Given that they are totally dependent on the Tremere for their very existence at this point, I sort of wish they had Potence in place of Dominate, just to drive home that they are slaves. Their not having a third discipline prior to joining the Tremor always felt a little like a cop out, IMO. I'm not really wild about their weakness either.)<br /> <br /> Basarab: Dominate, Protean, Vicissitude = Dracula, I guess.<br /> <br /> (Again, this feels like one of those where the writers were working backwards from something. I think Dominate, Animalism and Vicissitude might've been a better selection. And again, I'm not entirely wild about the use of a real world family line.)<br /> <br /> Danislav: Auspex, Protean, Vicissitude + Shadow Lord Gifts = Annoying crossover.<br /> <br /> (I don't really mind the idea of a corrupted werewolf bloodline now sworn to the Tzimisce for reasons of power and revenge, but I think they should've had Auspex, Animalism and Protean, or Dominate, Animalism and Protean, and no Gift access.)<br /> <br /> Oprichiniki: Animalism, Obfuscate, Vicissitude (or Potence, depending on edition) = Russian secret police, sort of.<br /> <br /> (Another where I've never been wild about the name, as it feels a little like naming a family after the KGB or something.)<br /> <br /> Ruthvenski: Apparently never detailed.<br /> <br /> Vlaszy: Animalism, Potence, Presence = horse-riding warlords<br /> <br /> Narov: Auspex, Dominate, Vicissitude = ?<br /> <br /> Khavi: Animalism, Obfuscate, Vicissitude = Worms Under the Earth?<br /> <br /> <br /> Maybe there was some sort of assassin lineage who had Obfuscate, Vicissitude and Celerity?<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-03-2021, 11:44 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T11:14:44-07:00","id":1447238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446566#post1446566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could have Revenants age more like Dhampirs, in that the slowed ageing only kicks in at puberty. It makes things a little easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>I prefer that for them, puberty last 20 or more years. mwahahah<br /> <br /> This thread reminded me of the Ducheski, who I will now be using in the MtAs game I am planning. This thread also gave me an idea of using the slow aging of Revenants as a plot hook. Specifically, a PC will have been childhood friends with a member of the family, only to have the Revenant move away. Then the PCs meets them again and the PC in now in their late 20s or something, but the Revenant has not aged.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T12:59:36-07:00","id":1447250,"title":"","content":"I find using existing revenant families somewhat limiting, so I like to run with the idea that, with the information age out and about (and perhaps some 19th century eugenicists being interested in such things) Neonates and Ancillae are oft trying to breed their own revenant families as a hobby. Malkavians, Tremere, Toreador and Ventrue are particularly into the practice. The Tremere try spells, some try by forcefully creating Dhampirs, whilst others use their connections to borrow breeding stock or otherwise exchange people. Entire coteries may centre around a family. <br /> <br /> Of course, most princes are opposed to this practice, but there are a few who're very interested.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T19:13:40-07:00","id":1447318,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447204#post1447204\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But I've honestly always felt like this family was created - by the writer and developer out of game, not by vampires in game -</div> </div> </div> </div>I think all of the Revenant families are like that to a degree. It's one thing to have family line of dedicated servants (perhaps towards a specific &quot;founder&quot; and his lineage of childer and named after this founder), but the specialization of purposes and a set of Disciplines to reflect that is very much a gamist element instead of a story narrative. But hey, it's an RPG. Players want options, and most are primarily interested in mechanical/powers options, not flavor text background. So you give everyone family different powers. What happens if these families mate with one another? What discipline set is used? Who knows? Then there is the issue of all Ghouls having Potence, but few of the Revenant families having Potence. I also think some of the Disciplines don't fit well for them as a concept. I have no problem with vampires having Protean, but should the living have it even with vitae in their veins?<br /> <br /> There's a lot of issues with the idea of breeding families of permanent ghouls/something else that just get handwaved over. It's fine as long as you don't examine them. I am interested in Revenants more as flavor for chronicles, as opposed to something for PCs, so if I ever use them I'll likely make various changes to better fit how I think they <i>should</i> work in the setting. Probably a universal set of Disciplines thematically appropriate (can be more than 3), but restricted to them and perhaps topped a certain a level so they can't be stronger than experienced vampires. Perhaps give some of them access to Paths of Sorcery and/or Dark Thaumaturgy/Infernalism.<br /> <br /> Also, I have always thought it was in poor taste to associate real life families with Revenants. Stay with fictional dynasties please. Having the ruling family of Monaco be related to them, even distantly, doesn't work for me. It's like saying that pizza chain is also owned by them since it shares their name.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T20:29:15-07:00","id":1447324,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447250#post1447250\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I find using existing revenant families somewhat limiting, so I like to run with the idea that, with the information age out and about (and perhaps some 19th century eugenicists being interested in such things) Neonates and Ancillae are oft trying to breed their own revenant families as a hobby. Malkavians, Tremere, Toreador and Ventrue are particularly into the practice. The Tremere try spells, some try by forcefully creating Dhampirs, whilst others use their connections to borrow breeding stock or otherwise exchange people. Entire coteries may centre around a family.<br /> <br /> Of course, most princes are opposed to this practice, but there are a few who're very interested.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that Revenant Families should require a mixture of Koldunism, Ghouldom, and experimentation over the course of centuries. No one but the Tzimisce or Tremere should have been able to figure it out.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T20:35:17-07:00","id":1447327,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447324#post1447324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that Revenant Families should require a mixture of Koldunism, Ghouldom, and experimentation over the course of centuries. No one but the Tzimisce or Tremere should have been able to figure it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>feh. The Giovanni should have it worked out as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T20:44:56-07:00","id":1447328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Grumpy RPG Reviews</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447327#post1447327\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> feh. The Giovanni should have it worked out as well.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah but the Giovanni don't necessarily WANT to create Revenants. The need and desire to do would be limited to people with weird transvampirist beliefs.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T23:30:32-07:00","id":1447356,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447324#post1447324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that Revenant Families should require a mixture of Koldunism, Ghouldom, and experimentation over the course of centuries. No one but the Tzimisce or Tremere should have been able to figure it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Selective breeding is more &quot;horrifying&quot; and appropriate I think. Just imagine a Ventrue or Malkavian calls in some favours to get the oldest ghouls in the city to come to his place and start a breeding program. Vampires treating humans like dogs, looking for a prized creation. <br /> Or a Toreador embraces a man to embrace a man until you get to thinblood territory, kills the uneccessary, and then starts a horrendous breeding program with the thinbloods and resulting Dhampirs. <br /> <br /> That's horror right there. That's a character your PCs will want to curbstomp. A Tremere/Koldun is too safe/humane, relatively speaking. When you have a vampire bloodbinding eight pregnant women eager to give birth to please the master, you've got horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-03T23:44:17-07:00","id":1447359,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447356#post1447356\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Selective breeding is more &quot;horrifying&quot; and appropriate I think. Just imagine a Ventrue or Malkavian calls in some favours to get the oldest ghouls in the city to come to his place and start a breeding program. Vampires treating humans like dogs, looking for a prized creation.<br /> Or a Toreador embraces a man to embrace a man until you get to thinblood territory, kills the uneccessary, and then starts a horrendous breeding program with the thinbloods and resulting Dhampirs.<br /> <br /> That's horror right there. That's a character your PCs will want to curbstomp. A Tremere/Koldun is too safe/humane, relatively speaking. When you have a vampire bloodbinding eight pregnant women eager to give birth to please the master, you've got horror.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think that is implied with the Tzimisce anyway. The problem is the more you make Revenants more common, the more you have to wonder how people haven't noticed versus even vampires.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T01:20:39-07:00","id":1447369,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447324#post1447324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think that Revenant Families should require a mixture of Koldunism, Ghouldom, and experimentation over the course of centuries. No one but the Tzimisce or Tremere should have been able to figure it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>I definitely think there should be some kind of &quot;secret&quot; involved in their creation. And lots of time. Lots and lots of time. Something that was plentiful back in the Iron Age of whenever they were first created. But something lacking in the modern era when there are far fewer isolated communities that could be left alone for bizarre inbred experiments by horrific undead masters. World is just too crowded now. Although I would substitute the True Black Hand for the Tremere since they're the ones with the most canonical Revnants after the Tzimisce.<br /> <br /> If it was easy to create them, there'd be far more than what exists, and I think would lead to a different setting than what we're used to. One more fantasy than based on the real world.<br /> <br /> Personally I like the idea that the &quot;secret&quot; as it were was lost to the Tzimisce during the Anarch Revolt. All those killings of elders had their consequences. I also think it would explain why the Sabbat Tzimisce subsequently committed to the remaining Revenant families preservation. They knew they couldn't create more if the remaining ones were lost. The True Black Hand might still possess the &quot;secret&quot;, but they face the same practical limitations of the modern ages, so it's not really feasible to create more Revenant families.<br /> <br /> If you go by Dirty Secrets of the Black Hand, it seems to be implied the Tzimisce learned how to create revenants as a result of their clan members' participation in that sect. Presumably the Sabbat revenant families and their Tzimisce creators left or were expelled by the sect when they became &quot;infected&quot; with the Souleaters/Vicissitude. Leaving only the &quot;original&quot; revenant families within the True Black Hand. (If so, then it becomes plausible that the True Black Hand members inside the Sabbat orchestrated the purging of the unknown families in order to eliminate the disease.) And Revenant creation seems dependent on non-vampire magic, since it required the participation of the Verbena within the sect. And now, that is no longer an option. Of course, so much of DSotBH is now retconned away who knows what the canonical answer is in other editions. But we have lots of evidence that originally it was not intended for revenant families to be easily created. There was secret sauce in the mystery meat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3046,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","user":"Black Fox","date":". 05-05-2021, 02:35 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T18:17:12-07:00","id":1447504,"title":"","content":"A tangental bit that I only have some vague recollection of at the moment: I think it may have been mentioned at least once of a revenant character having a name different than the family name, with that particular line being Anglicized or something. It's entirely possible I'm getting this confused with a Giovanni character from somewhere. I wish I could remember the character I'm thinking about and where I saw them. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T18:54:56-07:00","id":1447510,"title":"","content":"I\u2019ve long thought that it would make a lot of sense for the Giovanni to have revenant families, or even for the Giovanni themselves to be Revenants. In the V20 Ghouls and Revenants book, they made the Rossellini family Revenants, which I liked quite a bit, though I would also make Revenant families for the Giovanni themselves as well as the Dunsirn and a few of the other subfamilies. <br /> <br /> I also liked the attention shown to the non-Tzimisce families. I would agree with those who say that some kind of magic or Vicissitude shouldn\u2019t be necessary to create Revenants, only creepy breeding practices.<br /> <br /> A few years back, a friend and I created this alternative version of the WoD where the vampires all disappeared sometime in the Middle Ages and their place in the world was replaced with Revenany families. Who had their own political structures without vampires and we also changed the rules for Revenants a bit as well.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-04T20:51:44-07:00","id":1447520,"title":"","content":"I like to think the Revenant status surprised the Giovanni and they, and the families tied to them, don't keep track of everyone who is a revenant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1208-grumpy-rpg-reviews","name":"Grumpy RPG Reviews","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1208_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T08:53:41-07:00","id":1447581,"title":"","content":"The Giovanni aren't likely to accidentally create revenants because only so few of them are ghouled, and who's ghouled and what they do is something very tightly controlled. More to the point, the Giovanni are more interested in death than life, and a breeding program might satisfy some of their sick fuckery, but it'd also require a lot of dedication, which tends to be beyond the scope of a degenerate, and a lot of, well, abuse, and not many Giovanni are going to like the idea of abusing their own families, or at least they'd be to scared too abuse someone else's (and giovanni familes are very complicated) <br /> <br /> @CTphipps<br /> Revenants are rare, and if you doubled their number, they'd still be really really rare. More crucially all revenant families are going to have a vampire backer that can solve most of such problems, and most of them are going to have a Tzmisce backer that can give each member a &quot;facelift&quot; if necessary. There are plenty of barriers to entry into the world of making revenants; Princes would likely clamp down on projects not sanctioned, and it'd take a long while to actually get a revenant family up and running given how long it takes for revenants to grow up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T14:27:58-07:00","id":1447630,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447318#post1447318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I also think some of the Disciplines don't fit well for them as a concept. I have no problem with vampires having Protean, but should the living have it even with vitae in their veins?</div> </div> </div> </div>At least since Revised that's helped a good bit by the limit on how many dots of a discipline ghouls and revenants can learn without being boosted by low-generation vitae. Being able to see in the dark is a lot less jarring than turning into a bat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T18:35:43-07:00","id":1447677,"title":"","content":"I honestly don\u2019t think that many vampires would even want Revenants. I like the idea of creepy practices, available to most vampires, as the method of Revenant creation, but I don\u2019t think many vampires would do that.<br /> <br /> Taking populations of humans and ghouls and controlling their breeding over the course of generations is hard work. It takes persistence over the course of decades or centuries. It may be something that many vampires of high to moderate humanity might feel uncomfortable with. <br /> <br /> Beyond that, what benefit do you get from breeding humans like animals? Just finding the best humans around (people in positions of power, intelligent people, charismatic people, good fighters) and blood binding them is so convenient and that human as already done the hard work of becoming effective servants for you. Plus, these ghouls are completely obedient to you through the blood bond.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, creepy Revenants are a masquerade breech, are actually removed from the mainstream of humanity and so are probably less effective servants than just ghoulish noteworthy humans. Revenants have their own powers and longevity independently of vampires, they don\u2019t need to drink your blood and may not be blood bond, they aren\u2019t necessarily under as much control. They might be too independent, as history has shown with some families.<br /> <br /> I just don\u2019t think that it\u2019s worth it for the vast majority of vampires. Creating a Revenant family will be a labor of love, which is why you need a weird Clan like the Tzimisce to do it.<br /> <br /> As for why I think that the Giovanni might be inclined to do it. They care a lot about their family, the family itself is the center of the Clan. They frequently ghoul their family members and likely the Giovanni family humans are already enmeshed in the supernatural world, perhaps even learning Necromancy as mortals just as their ancestors did. They are also very creepy and inbred. The entire Clan screams Revenant to me as do some of the related families like the Rossellini (who are canon Revenants) and the Dunsirn who are a cannibal family that would fit right in with Revenants.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1129-sergeant-brother","name":"Sergeant Brother","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1129_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1620"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T19:16:04-07:00","id":1447682,"title":"","content":"To me, the Giovanni strive to hard to keep up appearances and work too much in the open to want to have Revenants. Yeah, they actually engage in some despicable and degenerate things, but that by itself does not fit the Revenant theme. There needs to be more. The only example of Revenants that appear &quot;normal&quot; would be the Grimaldi, and as pointed out by some here don't fit the vibe of the rest of the Revenants, and there are practical issues mixing with mortals because of the aging thing.<br /> <br /> But I do understand why someone feels the Giovanni would also have Revenant families, or association with some would be fitting.<br /> <br /> As for the Rossellini Revenants being canon, this is an example of canon changing. They weren't originally Revenants. Canon is very inspiring and fun to talk about, but at this point it's like one person is talking about Golden Age Batman, another about Silver Age Batman, one about post-Crisis Batman, and so on. While they all have certain elements in common, they are actually 3 (actually more now with even more reboots) different Batmans with no canon between them. It's not like the Sherlock Holmes stories where we can easily say the Doyle stories constitutes canon, and no matter how enjoyable some of the imitators may be, they are pastiches and not canon stories. That's how I feel between 1e/2e and Revised and V5. All are official, but they are different versions of the core setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-05T19:21:20-07:00","id":1447683,"title":"","content":"Getting back to the &quot;communities&quot; topic specifically and not just general comments on Revenants, do people have ideas or examples of a full blown Zantosa estate (where they raise horses for racing, to sell for ranch/farm work, and to eat horseflesh), or a Bratovitch estate in the hill country?<br /> <br /> It's great we all know the fiction we think fits for them - Addams family, Hills Have Eyes, Texas Chainsaw Massacre, Dunwich, etc. But in chronicles, we need something specific and fitting. So rather than just discuss theory, I'd be interested in hearing anything someone has actually done for one of their chronicles, or might have created on the fly after being inspired by this thread.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-06T12:02:01-07:00","id":1447723,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447682#post1447682\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me, the Giovanni strive to hard to keep up appearances and work too much in the open to want to have Revenants.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think a more interesting angle with the Giovanni would be that rather than deliberately creating Revenants, all their inbreeding and ghouling of family did it accidentally and in something of a cascade effect where before they even realized it was happening half the kids in a given human generation were just aging REALLY slowly... and so now the Giovanni are basically &quot;Fuck! What do we do with THIS?&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1328-chris24601","name":"Chris24601","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"520"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-10T23:38:25-07:00","id":1448204,"title":"","content":"I assume the wilderness around a Bratovich estate would be booby-trapped to a degree that would make Central American drug kingpins green with envy. Or perhaps located in a bayou/swamp where their facility with Animalism would allow natural equivalents in the form of gators, venomous snakes, and other wildlife dangerous to trespassers but not family members.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-11T12:19:04-07:00","id":1448252,"title":"","content":"Good ideas. Booby traps and places where dangerous animals can be used to most advantage both fit very well.<br /> <br /> I had not thought about swamps and wetlands before as a fitting home for the Bratovitch, but it makes a lot of sense now that I think about it. You can get a lot more use of out gators, venomous snakes, constrictors, and the like since they are mostly not seen in a swamp and there can be a lot of them, than you can from wildlife in other desolate areas like mountains or deserts because there's a lot less pumas and other large, dangerous predators. They'd have to rely more on domesticated animals. Though now that I have seen Tiger King on Netflix, I'm open to the possibilities that the Bratovitch run an unregulated exotic animal &quot;zoo&quot;/&quot;animal rescue&quot;/breeding program that gives them access to a lot of dangerous animals they control in other areas.<br /> <br /> So there's probably some Bratovitch estates on the Gulf Coast and Florida. Since Miami is supposed to have been contested domain for the Sabbat, it makes sense for the Bratovitch to be found in the nearby Everglades. They can easily support the Sabbat there. I don't think the Sabbat has traditionally been found near New Orleans or the Gulf Coast, but maybe some place far from New Orleans there is an estate that can be used for nomadic Sabbat packs to use as a safe stopping point in their travels. There might be similar spots in Mexico the Bratovitch could go, but I think it'd be harder for them to fit in as the most likely spots are heavily Indian and they'd stick out more.<br /> <br /> Since there's supposed to be around 20 Bratovitch estates (all in North America I think), we could easily place 4-8 of them in such wetlands and still leave a large number in mountains, hills, and other desolate areas. Are there good large swamp areas in Michigan, Pennsylavia, Maine, and the rest of northern Eastern Seaboard that would be good locations for Bratovitch to support the Sabbat there?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-18T09:50:07-07:00","id":1448876,"title":"","content":"Late to the party but I had some stuff I wanted to add: <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities#post1446544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious how people describe or detail Revenant homesteads, particularly the Grimaldi and Obertus.<br /> <br /> We're told that Zantosa and Bratovitch live in family estates. Bratovitches live on around 20 with 10-20 members each. So there's around 300 Bratovitch's total. Zantosa live on &quot;at least&quot; 17 estates between 5-20 members. So maybe around 200 plus members. Large, isolated family estates easily allow these Revenants to do whatever they want outside the eyes of mortals.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly I think the numbers were meant to be kept low but as with most things population wise in WoD it can depend on what sources you listen to. Its very much a YMMV thing so there could be more. I mean its not as if they didn't keep adding more Revenant families after the fact (indeed Revenants have gone through some revisions through different editions I've noticed.) <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the Grimaldi and Obertus are different and present some challenges for the ST.<br /> <br /> Grimaldi are supposed to live among humanity, but as individuals or presumably nuclear family units. The problem is that revenants age slowly. Not an issue for adults, but children are supposed to take 50 years to reach adulthood. It's possible for people to interact with an adult for a long time without noticing they don't age normally. You can probably spend one to decades in the same place before moving on. But if children don't age, it'll be noticed quickly. That does not present a problem for Zantosa and Bratovitch who live isolated from humans, and can do various things to limit their interaction with them. Much harder for the Grimaldi. How do you handle this in the setting as the ST?<br /> <br /> Obertus have another issue. They do seem to live in isolation from humanity, but instead of living on family estates they gather in villages and even govern themselves &quot;normally&quot; like having municipal elections and such. The issue here isn't hiding among humanity. They probably keep outsiders out of their communities and create any false documents as needed like birth and death certificates. The issue is given the small number of Revenants, the Obertus likely number in the same range as Bratvotiches and Zantosa. Let's be generous and give them 500. How many villages can they possibly have? We're told they can be found in New England, the Midwest, and Central America. Does that mean all Obertus live in a total of three villages - one in each of those regions - of around 150-170 members each? It's possible to have villages this size, but they are very rare. There wouldn't be many places of business or public buildings. You could still theoretically elect a village president or mayor, and even a council, but they wouldn't have a whole lot to do. We can make it work, but the greater issue is should there really only be three villages total to account for the entirety of this revenant family - let's call them Dunwich, Gatlin, and Mayincateca for convenience. I imagine in addition to being remote, they are also isolated by geography in someway to prevent outsiders from stumbling upon them by accident and making the region around them unattractive for development. As home to around one-third of the whole revenant family (as opposed to just a clan) you can't just pick up and flee. You have to make yourself a hard target. Is that how you see it in your chronicles? </div> </div> </div>I never saw it as a problem honestly but I think it could depend on how your story is set up. The key thing (in my mind) to remember is that most Revenant families are only kept around (apart from habit) but because they have highly specialized roles. Grimaldi have their connections to the mortal world (And money) and the Obertus have alot of esoteric knowledge and research abilities. Grimaldi can be 'private' schooled or even privately tutored (Ghouls: Fatal addiction mentions Revenants are never in public school of any kind for more than a few years at a time, so you could imagine they get shuffled around and anyone asks says they got sent to boarding school or something) but even with that Revenants tend to be 'insular' and 'clannish'. There's lots of ways to be standoffish without drawing attention even for Obertuses ('those old religious recluses/crazy cultists several miles from town way up on the hill' for example would suit Obertuses) G:FA also mentions sometimes that fleshcrafting may be used to 'disguise' Revenants which can also help. <br /> <br /> I'd also imagine that any Tzimisce or Sabbat who cared enough can arrange for special 'private' schools that can help conceal Revenant's true natures or otherwise take steps to isolate them. Hell some Revenants like the Grimaldis probably can do this for themselves (or others) already. <br /> <br /> Plus I've always figured any Revenant family will have not inconsiderable amounts of land and wealth. Even the Obertus need lots of land to store their records, libraries and whatnot and you have to pay the upkeep (and acquisition of new stuff) somehow. And since the rise of online schooling (or more recently virtual schooling) to complement 'traditional' excuses like home schooling or boarding schools there's lots of ways you can conceal the truth. And this assumes any one Revenant gets enough contact with mortal society for a long enough period for anyone to notice, which I kinda doubt. If you own a amount of land outside the city (and its walled off) nobody is going to get close enough to notice anything. And even close to urban areas you can have gated communities or walled off houses (usually again the wealthy) so its not THAT out of place to have people who aren't in regular contact with people. And Revenants who aren't Grimaldi aren't supposed to be normal/sane enough to interact with people anyhow so secrecy would be necessary regardless of age. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446566#post1446566\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You could have Revenants age more like Dhampirs, in that the slowed ageing only kicks in at puberty. It makes things a little easier.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually while Revenants age slowly even through childhood and puberty, Dhampir powers don't kick in until Puberty as per Time of Thin Blood<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Time of Thin Blood</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A dhampir's occult legacy usually doesn't manifest until the onset of puberty; in addition to all the usual tribulations of growing up, the child must face a second layer of transformation, far darker and infinitely more mysterious.<br /> <br /> The process varies from individual to individual. Most simply become aware, sometime during their teens, of a special reserve of physical strength that they can call upon - instinctively at first, in times of stress.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Other dhampirs bloom later. For them, mystical dabbling or a sudden encounter with a vampire might be what it takes to spark their hidden potential. <br /> <br /> In any case, once a dhampir is fully awakened to her supernatural essence, she can never go back. The Blood forever alters her physically and mentally. Her aging slows to a crawl (which can be a real social disadvantage when it happens in early puberty). </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> In other words, as Dr. Netchurch so astutely notes in Chapter One, she becomes biologically indistinguishable from a revenant.<br /> <br /> (Note: <i>Biologically</i> is the all-important qualifier. In all other respects, dhampirs could not be more diffrent from the freakish Bratovitches, Zantosas, et al. Revenants have a culture of their own, a skewed outlook that they have developed over many centuries. Dhampirs nominally belong to the human world, however imperfect the fit may be.) </div> </div> </div>And the reference to Netchurch from chapter one: <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It appears, therefore, that two processes may generate revenants. They may be \u201cbred up\u201d from ghouls. Alternatively, revenants may be \u201cbred down\u201d from vampires. For reasons I shall explain below, I believe this can happen only among vampires of the 15th Generation.<br /> <br /> To prevent confusion, such offspring ought to have a distinctive name of their own. The Gypsies of the Balkan region have legends of such creatures; they call them dhampirs. Although these legends are surely mere coincidence \u2014 perhaps even the result of old disinformation to protect the Masquerade \u2014 I propose using this name for the living offspring of vampires. It is a name we shall need: Michael may well be the first dhampir, but as the thin-blooded proliferate, he will not be the last. </div> </div> </div>I should note that in both mechanics and lore while Dhampir and Revenants are similar they're not identical. Revenants can (technically) learn true magic, whereas a Dhampir can't ever do that (or be any other kind of hybrid. They're pretty damn explicit about this even if they're 'technically' like Revenants.) They also don't have 'clan' disciplines (they're more like Revenant Caitiff in that regard). <br /> <br /> But the biggest difference is in genesis. As Netchurch notes you can 'breed down' from Thin Bloods or 'breed up' from ghouls. Additionally the former is a 'natural' result of the Curse of Caine whereas the Revenants were an artificially created 'mutation' of the Curse so naturally there will be differences (Dhampir's don't 'activate' until they reach a certain trigger point, whereas Revenants are 'activated' from the start because of how they were created.) <br /> <br /> Honestly I kinda wish they managed things better than 'Mostly Revenants' but you get what you get (as opposed to say, the Shade Walkers in KotE which are fundamentally the same concept.) <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malkavian87</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446581#post1446581\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is it stated somewhere that revenants age slower from birth? Cause I could have sworn I read a reference last year to this only starting in early adulthood. Having no success finding a direct statement to that effect in Ghouls &amp; Revenants though. What I did find however is a Bratovitch sample character being described as having grown to 6 feet by age 10. He's mentioned on page 117.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yup. Page 22 of Ghouls: Fatal Addiction.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ghouls Fatal Addiction</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Simply put, the retarded cell growth and decay afforded by their vitae similarly affects their young. The gestation period I observed in one Bratovitch mother lasted for three years, roughly one year per trimester. It takes a revenant child almost 50 years to reach puberty, from what I understand. Obviously, such children are never reared in public schools for more than a few years at a time. Presumably, a fleshcrafting patron may change the outward form to give a cosmetic appearance of adulthood (grafting on flesh if needed), but the endocrine system bestows true maturity at its own pace.<br /> <br /> The psychological effects of such a long childhood are equally fascinating, but I believe my assistant is more qualified to explore such a conundrum.</div> </div> </div> </div>Basically as I said above WRT Dhampir Revenants seem born with their supernatural nature 'always on' so that slowed growth affects all other biological processes including birth. It also contributes to their low birthrate (a weaker form of the problem that afflicts ghouls and complicated the means by which Revenants were created, after all.) <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1446881#post1446881\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When Revenants were introduced we were told that original 8 families were loyal to the Sabbat, but the Sabbat eventually destroyed four of them before the Tzimisce put the survivors under their own protection. But I don't think we've ever been told who those other 4 families were or the circumstances surrounding their destruction.<br /> <br /> Eventually we got a lot more Revenant families named during the Tzimisce clanbooks and Dark Ages line, but so far as I know, none of them ever had a destiny of entering the Sabbat and being destroyed by them. They were either destroyed while never entering the Sabbat, created afterwards, or joined some other faction.<br /> <br /> Did I just miss that somewhere, or has the game never defined them?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think like most things, the 'design' of Revenants changed or evolved over time. They show up in some pre-revised materials (like the early Tal'mahe're' supplement because the Hand had its own Revenant families) and you find out that Revenants could live to be over 800+ years old (and at least one Revenant had a second dot in a discipline although no rules I recall were given.) I recall seeing MET rules in the past for Revenants that also gave them 'higher than basic' powers, but MET runs on a different system than the D10 one so that could be at play. You see similar issues with Dhampir (compare Time of Thin Blood to KOTE Companion or the 'Dhampyr' book to see differences in how the concept is developed... which itself has some overtones which I suspect reflect what earlier Revenants may have been intended to be.) <br /> <br /> In that vein I suspect how many familes (and again population) changed over time as the game evolved and grew. AFAIK every family known up to now has been included on the wiki (see <a href=\"https://whitewolf.fandom.com/wiki/Revenant_(VTM)\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>.) and there are quite a few by now. <br /> <br /> Also of interest WRT Revenant abilities. There's one Dark Ages Revenant Clan that descends from Shadow Lord kinfolk (enslaved by the Tzimisce and long extinct supposedly) named the Danislavs. They could use some Shadow Lord gifts (up to the 3rd level) and as I recall powered them by blood rather than Gnosis. Such is the convoluted development of the hybrids.) <br /> <br /> \u200b<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>CTPhipps</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1447324#post1447324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think that Revenant Families should require a mixture of Koldunism, Ghouldom, and experimentation over the course of centuries. No one but the Tzimisce or Tremere should have been able to figure it out.</div> </div> </div> </div>With that approach some people might find the later (not earlier) VtR's approach to dhampir more suitable since as I recall (Albeit vaguely) that alot of magic was involved in the creation there and the home life was kind of screwy (so was some of the art lol). Sort of halfway between VtM Dhampir and Werewolves. <br /> <br /> If one doesn't mind the Hellsing-esque analogies you could also look at Revenants being 'artificial duskborn' or Dhampir (I tend to assume at this point that Dhampir are part of a larger spectrum of duskborn that include the former thinbloods as part of an inverse relationship between their humanity and vampiric natures.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25533-mister_dunpeal","name":"Mister_Dunpeal","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25533_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"308"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-18T22:23:43-07:00","id":1448939,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1448876#post1448876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I never saw it as a problem honestly but I think it could depend on how your story is set up.</div> </div> </div> </div>By problem I meant that it was something STs need to take into consideration when they are presenting those families. Unlike the other Revenant families, Grimaldi don't live by themselves, but among humans. That present challenges because certain aspects of Revenants would reveal them to be non-humans. In my experience, this is often overlooked by STs so I want to emphasize it. Or if you do things that isolate them, it goes against what the Grimaldi are supposed to be like. Some may be inevitable - like special boarding school they send their &quot;children&quot; off to before they decide to integrate them in human society by sending them off to college (more for the purpose of networking since they likely don't need the actual education at that point). But most attempts of the Grimaldi to self-isolate would be self-defeating. <br /> <br /> But now that you figure out the Grimaldi need a special boarding school to keep the &quot;kids&quot; away from humans (given their small numbers, I think there should be only one), now you have something you can put in your game world and maybe it becomes a cool element in your chronicles. Maybe even the Zantosa sometimes send their kids to there, and that's one reason for these families rivalries. The Zantosa on their estates don't need such a school, but maybe it can provide some benefits to help them socialize and learn so they're better prepared when they do walk among the humans. It's ideas like that STs can use to improve their games as they start to inspire more things.<br /> <br /> In terms of the Obertus, they don't have the problems of the Grimaldi since they are isolated. But their low numbers necessitates that there only be a small number of their towns/villages. You just can't place them anywhere if it seems there are only 3 such communities in the whole world. There are just certain things that don't seem obvious until you begin to figure out the details. There's a difference between &quot;they can be found in New England&quot; and &quot;Haverhill, MA is one of only three Obertus communities in the world.&quot; As you said, there is an implied population increase in most supernatural creatures since once informed of any group, STs and PCs begin using those concepts and creating their own details. I don't think there's any problem deviating from canon, but I prefer such deviations to be an active design decision by the ST rather than them just being ignorant (which given the sheer number of sourcebooks now, some of which are contradictory can't be helped).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mister_Dunpeal</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1446544-revenant-communities?p=1448876#post1448876\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think like most things, the 'design' of Revenants changed or evolved over time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Definitely. I've said before on the forum that at this point, we have three separate canons of the WoD like the old Earth-1, Earth-2, etc. of DC Comics. Although all based on the same core material, the Greenberg 1e/2e VtM, the Revised metaplot of Achilli, and now V5 just have too many retcons and changes to be one continuity anymore. And the Revenants are not immune to this. That 2e statement about 4 extra families that entered the Sabbat and were destroyed seems to be a 2e element ignored, forgotten, or changed in the Dark Ages/Revised era. I'm sure when V5 delves more into the Sabbat, we will &quot;learn&quot; more things about the Revenant families that contradict earlier books.<br /> <br /> In the end, every ST will be deciding what is to their taste and cobbling things together that best match that while cutting off the bits that don't. I do think a thread like this helps identify various aspects and helps people think what it is they do like.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}